  <!-- Jungle: a shelf fungus on the forest floor, dark magenta going red.

       Fungal rather than the broadleaf first drafted, because the tile is Brightshroom's and a
       plant should match the tile it borrows rather than the other way round (#540).

       &m against a canopy that is almost entirely &g - swarmshade and ziv bough both - and ripe &R
       is the largest hue jump of the three, which a jungle floor needs. Brightshroom shares the
       tile at &Y and is not mistakable for it.

       Inherits Fungus, not Plant, and that is the whole of #860. The two are siblings under
       PhysicalObject and near-identical, so inheriting the wrong one is invisible until something
       reads a value: Category="Plants" on the Physics part, PlantProperties instead of
       FungusProperties, the Plant/LivePlant tags, BodyType="Bush", plant damage and death sounds,
       and FiberMaterial="bark" - a shelf fungus building a village wall out of bark, which is the
       exact defect FEATURES §18.5 opens by describing on a reed. Vanilla never hits it because
       Fungus inherits PhysicalObject and carries Plank="mass" and nothing else, so there is no
       fungal FiberMaterial in the game to override with. It declines the question; the fix is to
       stop asking it.

       The Plank="mass" override is gone with the parent change: Fungus already gives exactly that,
       so restating it would be a value nobody could tell was inherited. -->
  <object Name="Vixy_Broadglove" Inherits="Fungus">
    <part
      Name="Render"
      DisplayName="broadglove"
      RenderString="005"
      ColorString="&amp;m"
      TileColor="&amp;m"
      DetailColor="w"
      Occluding="false"
      Tile="Terrain/sw_shroom_caps_spotted_1.bmp"
    />
    <builder
      Name="RandomTile"
      Tiles="Terrain/sw_shroom_caps_spotted_1.bmp,Terrain/sw_shroom_caps_spotted_2.bmp,Terrain/sw_shroom_caps_spotted_3.bmp,Terrain/sw_shroom_caps_spotted_4.bmp"
    />
    <part
      Name="Harvestable"
      OnSuccess="Vixy_Broadglove Cap"
      StartRipeChance="1:24"
      RipeColor="&amp;R"
      RipeTileColor="&amp;R"
      RipeDetailColor="W"
      UnripeColor="&amp;m"
      UnripeTileColor="&amp;m"
      UnripeDetailColor="w"
    />
    <part
      Name="Description"
      Short="It shelves out from anything that has stopped growing, in caps wide enough to turn rain off a hand held under them. The flesh is bitter raw and keeps that bitterness through drying, which is the point of it."
    />
    <tag Name="DynamicObjectsTable:Jungle_Plants" />
  </object>
  <!-- Saltdunes: a low, fine-cut plant on the salt flats, dark blue going white as it ripens.

       &b against a ground that is &Y, &y and &c across SaltDune, HighSaltDuneWhite and
       HighSaltDuneCyan, and against Fracti's &g - the biome's only other plant. Dark on pale is the
       inverse of every other plant here, and deliberate: §18.4b's lesson is that a plant you cannot
       see is a plant that is not there, and on a white flat the visible thing is the dark one.

       Ripe is &b to &W, a hue change rather than a brightness bump, and it is the plant's own
       biology rather than a colour picked to be legible: halophytes push salt out through their
       leaves, so a ripe one wears a crust. Fracti ripens &g to &M and is not mistakable for it.

       Fracti's stag set, the salt flats' only named neighbour: strip / fibre / strip. Fiber is left
       inherited because 'strip' is already the default.

       The tile is sw_flowers_bunched, and the first draft was `sw_brush_2.bmp`, which does not
       exist - I invented a plausible name rather than taking one the game already uses, and Qud
       renders a missing tile as a solid coloured block. Nothing static caught it: a tile name is a
       string nothing validates, and it only showed up by walking the salt flats. §18.4b is the
       neighbouring lesson - a plant that renders wrong is a plant that is not there.

       sw_flowers_bunched has four variants and exactly one user in vanilla, the `Flowers` item you
       carry. So nothing *growing* in any biome looks like this, which is what makes it safe to
       borrow: finger-root belongs to Nails of Jy, a unique, and sw_coral_branching to Finger
       Coral. -->
  <object Name="Vixy_Dunelace" Inherits="Plant">
    <part
      Name="Render"
      DisplayName="dunelace"
      RenderString="171"
      ColorString="&amp;b"
      TileColor="&amp;b"
      DetailColor="c"
      Occluding="false"
      Tile="Terrain/sw_flowers_bunched_1.bmp"
    />
    <builder
      Name="RandomTile"
      Tiles="Terrain/sw_flowers_bunched_1.bmp,Terrain/sw_flowers_bunched_2.bmp,Terrain/sw_flowers_bunched_3.bmp,Terrain/sw_flowers_bunched_4.bmp"
    />
    <part
      Name="Harvestable"
      OnSuccess="Vixy_Dunelace Frill"
      StartRipeChance="1:4"
      RipeColor="&amp;W"
      RipeTileColor="&amp;W"
      RipeDetailColor="c"
      UnripeColor="&amp;b"
      UnripeTileColor="&amp;b"
      UnripeDetailColor="c"
    />
    <part
      Name="Description"
      Short="It cuts itself into threads to keep what little it drinks, and lays them flat against the crust so the wind has nothing to take hold of. A ripe one wears the salt it has pushed back out, and shows white from a long way off."
    />
    <tag Name="DynamicObjectsTable:Saltdunes_Plants" />
    <stag Name="FiberMaterial" Value="fibre" />
    <stag Name="Plank" Value="strip" />
  </object>

  <!-- Ruins overgrowth (#173). Decoration only: no Harvestable, nothing to gather, nothing to
       spend. These are the first two blueprints in this file that give a player nothing at all,
       and that is the point - the whole issue is scoped so that no number a player can act on
       moves.

       Passable, because Plant is Solid="false" and neither of these overrides it. The design doc
       allowed overgrowth to block movement as unavoidable for a physical object; it turns out to
       be entirely avoidable, so it is avoided.

       RenderLayer stays at Plant's 3, deliberately. Anything above 5 makes Cell.IsEmpty() false,
       which would stop two ever sharing a cell - and would also change where a player may pitch a
       camp or lay a mine, how gas spreads, and a dozen mutations' targeting. Placement hints do
       that job instead, at no cost (#542).

       No RenderIfDark either, though Grave Moss carries it. Seeing terrain in an unlit room is
       information, and vanilla's own ruins vegetation does without it. -->

  <!-- Verdigris on old fulcrete. Teal because everything already growing in a ruin is &g or &G
       green - swarmshade, ziv bough, star palm, starapple - and a fourth green would read as more
       canopy rather than as something on the floor. The tile is Grave Moss's five-tile mat. -->
  <object Name="Vixy_Slabmoss" Inherits="Plant">
    <part
      Name="Render"
      DisplayName="slabmoss"
      RenderString="231"
      ColorString="&amp;c"
      TileColor="&amp;c"
      DetailColor="C"
      Occluding="false"
      Tile="Creatures/sw_moss_1.bmp"
    />
    <builder
      Name="RandomTile"
      Tiles="Creatures/sw_moss_1.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_2.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_3.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_4.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_5.bmp"
    />
    <part
      Name="Description"
      Short="It takes the damp out of a slab and gives back a blue-green crust that squeaks underfoot. Where a roof has gone it stops at the rain line, so the old rooms map themselves out in it."
    />
    <stag Name="Plank" Value="mass" />
  </object>

  <!-- Hanging growth banked against masonry. Green, and green is right here: a ruin's vegetation
       is green, and the shape carries the difference - vertical strands against the moss mat's
       speckle. The tile is yuckwheat's. Placed AlongWall rather than LivesOnWalls, because
       LivesOnWalls sets flag3 in PlaceObjectInArea and skips the same-blueprint check, which is
       exactly the defect #542 removed. -->
  <object Name="Vixy_Pallvine" Inherits="Plant">
    <part
      Name="Render"
      DisplayName="pallvine"
      RenderString="245"
      ColorString="&amp;g"
      TileColor="&amp;g"
      DetailColor="G"
      Occluding="false"
      Tile="Terrain/sw_wheat_2.bmp"
    />
    <builder Name="RandomTile" Tiles="Terrain/sw_wheat_2.bmp,Terrain/sw_wheat_3.bmp,Terrain/sw_wheat_4.bmp" />
    <part
      Name="Description"
      Short="It roots in the seam where a wall meets its floor and hangs from there in long dry ropes, so a room comes to be curtained from the edges inward. Pulling a handful down brings a century of dust with it."
    />
    <stag Name="Fiber" Value="twine" />
    <stag Name="FiberMaterial" Value="thatch" />
  </object>
  <!-- Cave overgrowth (#547), the same scope as the ruins pair: decoration, nothing to gather.

       It shares slabmoss's tile deliberately. Vanilla does this constantly - sw_talltree1 carries
       witchwood, the n-ary tree, glitchwood and the icosahedar, and sw_aloe_solo carries four aloes
       - and colour is what separates them. So this is not slabmoss stretched underground; it is the
       relationship witchwood and glitchwood already have.

       &m because it has to separate from everything a cave already grows: dreadroot at &K, young
       ivory at &Y over a &y tile, brightshroom the same, and slabmoss at &c. Magenta is also the
       game's register for lightless growth - noisegrass ripens &K to &M. &K was tried first and is
       nearly invisible against the floor, which is the cragwort mistake from #540 exactly. -->
  <object Name="Vixy_Bruisemoss" Inherits="Plant">
    <part
      Name="Render"
      DisplayName="bruisemoss"
      RenderString="231"
      ColorString="&amp;m"
      TileColor="&amp;m"
      DetailColor="M"
      Occluding="false"
      Tile="Creatures/sw_moss_1.bmp"
    />
    <builder
      Name="RandomTile"
      Tiles="Creatures/sw_moss_1.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_2.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_3.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_4.bmp,Creatures/sw_moss_5.bmp"
    />
    <part
      Name="Description"
      Short="It never sees light and has no use for the green that would gather it, so it comes up the colour of a week-old bruise. Press a thumb into a patch and the damp stands in the print for a while afterward."
    />
    <stag Name="Plank" Value="mass" />
  </object>
</objects>
